`week2/generated/day5-practice-isolation.spec.ts` — the point is to make it **fail**:

```ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";

const BANK = "https://test-automation-banking.vercel.app/";

test.describe("Pay Bills, split in two", () => {
  test("1 - navigate to Pay Bills", async ({ page }) => {
    await page.goto(BANK);
    await page.getByPlaceholder("Enter Email").fill("leela@gmail.com");
    await page.getByPlaceholder("Enter Password").fill("Leela@123");
    await page.getByRole("button", { name: "Login" }).click();
    await page.waitForURL("**/dashboard");

    await page.getByText("Pay Bills").click();
    await expect(page.getByText("Pay Bills")).toBeVisible();
  });

  test("2 - read Billing History, assuming test 1 got us there", async ({ page }) => {
    // No goto, no login. This test assumes it inherits test 1's browser.
    await page.getByText("Billing History").click();
    await expect(page.getByText("Billing History")).toBeVisible();
  });
});
```

```bash
npx playwright test week2/generated/day5-practice-isolation.spec.ts
```
→ **`1 failed, 1 passed`**.

**Read the failure carefully, because it is the lesson.** Test 2 fails waiting for a locator that is not there — and the trace shows why: it is sitting on `about:blank`. Not on the login page, not logged out. A completely fresh browser context that has never visited the app.

**The thing that surprises people:** `test.describe` groups tests for *reporting*. It does not share a browser between them. Every `test()` gets its own context and its own `page` — that is the isolation guarantee, and it is on by default precisely so one test's mess cannot explain another test's result.

Splitting a long test into two is the right instinct. Assuming the second inherits the first's state is the trap that comes with it.

The fix is one shared context, built once, with the tests running in order.

```ts
import { test, expect, type Page, type BrowserContext } from "@playwright/test";

const BANK = "https://test-automation-banking.vercel.app/";

test.describe.configure({ mode: "serial" });

test.describe("Pay Bills, sharing one context", () => {
  let context: BrowserContext;
  let page: Page;

  test.beforeAll(async ({ browser }) => {
    context = await browser.newContext();
    page = await context.newPage();

    await page.goto(BANK);
    await page.getByPlaceholder("Enter Email").fill("leela@gmail.com");
    await page.getByPlaceholder("Enter Password").fill("Leela@123");
    await page.getByRole("button", { name: "Login" }).click();
    await page.waitForURL("**/dashboard");
  });

  test.afterAll(async () => {
    await context.close();
  });

  test("1 - navigate to Pay Bills", async () => {
    await page.getByText("Pay Bills").click();
    await expect(page.getByText("Pay Bills")).toBeVisible();
  });

  test("2 - read Billing History", async () => {
    await page.getByText("Billing History").click();
    await expect(page.getByText("Billing History")).toBeVisible();
  });
});
```

→ **`2 passed`**.

**Four things changed, and all four are load-bearing.**

`browser.newContext()` creates one browser session the tests share. `beforeAll` builds it **once** for the file, not per test. `afterAll` closes it — without that the context leaks for the whole run. And `mode: "serial"` stops the two tests running in parallel, which would otherwise have them fighting over one page.

**Note what the tests no longer take.** No `{ page }` argument — they close over the shared `page` instead. Accept the fixture and you get a fresh isolated page again, which is the default you were working around.

**And the cost, which the lesson is honest about.** You have given up isolation deliberately. Test 2 now depends on test 1, so a failure in 1 cascades, and you cannot run 2 alone. That is an acceptable trade for a genuinely sequential journey and a bad habit everywhere else. Week 3 shows the version you reach for far more often: `storageState`, which gives every test a logged-in session *without* giving up isolation.
